>Buckminster Fuller presents the idea in his books (Critical Path, 
>Spaceship Earth, etc.) that limited liability is a primal evil.  On the 
>contrary, without it, capitalism is not possible and socialism a necessity.

Scotland underwent higher economic growth during the 17th and early 18th
centuries than England without it.  This indicates that it's less than
necessary, althogh it may still be desirable.

>There is no reason that investors should be responsible for torts.  

Sure there are.  One possible reason is to give them an incentive to try
to make sure that they invest in companies that have good records of
avoiding tort liability.  For instance, why should holders of U.S. Federal
Government bonds be able to escape the responsibility for the fact that
they're essentially investing in extortion futures?  Surely there is some
moral responsibility not to invest in that which can only be paid off with
blood money, isn't there?

>Surely a way can be found for the officers to assume these burdens and 
>buy liability insurance accordingly.

Yes, there are ways to do this, contractually, without State privilege.
There are two different things involved here: shareholder liability, and
the liability of the firm as a whole.  Shareholder liability can be limited
to the amount of their investment by contractual assumption of the extra
risk by another party, such as the managers of the firm or a liability
insurer.  What State-limited liability does, on the other hand, is limit
the liability of the firm as a whole to its assets.  This is essentially
a price ceiling in favor of corporations from the State.

>"Looking for Deep Pockets" when damages are due is not, in my opinion, an 
>extropian or libertarian principle.  Blame or responsibility should be 
>the issue.  Who made the decision that caused the damages?  For blameless 
>accidents no one would be forced to pay in a libertarian world.

I agree.

