From: freeman@netcom.com (Jay Reynolds Freeman)
Date: Mon, 12 Dec 1994 22:09:50 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-497] Plant communities -- terms and development

    If you start with an area of bare ground and watch for many years
as plants populated it, you get a changing pattern of abundances of
various species that is called a "succession".  "Primary succession"
is what happens when the ground in question has never had a plant
community -- new sand dunes or new vocanic flows are sites where this
happens.  "Secondary succession" occurs when previously-existing
vegetation has been stripped, as by a serious fire, perhaps leaving
nutrients in the soil that affect what happens next.  Different stages
in succession, in a particular area, often have descriptive names
based on what kind of vegetation is dominant at the time in question,
but in general there is often a trend from opportunistic species that
need lots of sun and grow fast, toward shade-tolerant species that
produce lots of shade and grow slowly.  There is a lot of argument as
to whether and how the successive plant communities in a particular
succession interact with one another.  Does the community at this time
pave the way for the next one (not suggesting a deliberate process),
or do plants in one stage inhibit their predecessors as they become
established, or what?

    If the process of succession is allowed to continue without
catastrophic interruption, it may arrive at a stable state, in which
the abundances of all remaining species are constant with time.  This
state, if it does exist, is called "climax".  A static climax need not
in principle exist, even in the absence of catastrophic interruption:
Clearly, dynamic equilibria are in principle possible.  And
furthermore, the locale in question may be subject to catastrophy with
sufficient frequency that the vegetation never makes it to climax.
Thus in the US western redwood forests, major fires happen too often
for the climax condition -- believed to be mixed spruce and hemlock,
not redwood -- ever to occur.  It would take perhaps tens of thousands
of fire-free years for a climax to be reached.  That's not to say that
redwood forests are young -- many of the old-growth trees logged off
were up in the thousands of years old.

    An interesting example of succession can be seen in most people's
lawns.  Dandelions are an opportunistic plant that is well adapted to
finding and growing on bare ground, or nearly bare ground.  The
wind-born seeds are good for being distributed widely, and the shape
of the plant itself is well adapted to a place where little else
grows.  Notice how a growing dandelion spreads out a "rosette" of
horizontal leaves that gather lots of sunlight, shade out competing
plants, and -- via the depressions and folds in the leaves -- route
rainfall in toward the central root of the plant.  That's a classic
example of a good early-succession plant, that will be competed out
just as soon as slower-growing plants get well enough established so
that the sprouting dandelion cannot spread out its rosette.  So if you
want to eliminate dandelions from your own lawn, by the most
ecologically sound and labor-saving of practices, all you have to do
is ... don't mow the grass!

                            -- Jay Freeman, First Extropian Squirrel

